{
	"version": 5,
	"rows": [
		{
			"id": "eNYnJE95xz2_3sXcq",
			"description": "Rebreather",
			"local_notes": "A light filter mask covering nose and mouth; grants a bonus to HT rolls to resist inhaled toxins, smoke and dust, but is not a full atmospheric seal.\n\nLore: Basic breathing protection issued wherever the Munitorum expects toxic atmospheres, ash storms or gas warfare short of a full hazmat environment — seen throughout Astra Militarum background art and Black Library fiction set on hive or forge worlds.\n\nDesign: No armour/damage anchor applies; built as a simple GURPS filter mask (a HT-roll bonus vs. inhaled hazards) at a TL appropriate to \"stub tech\"-era mass production, since a rebreather is mechanically simple and produced across the Imperium regardless of a world's exact tech base.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "8",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "60",
			"base_weight": "1 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e6bZUAqohbYaoG3Nj",
			"description": "Respirator",
			"local_notes": "A full sealed mask with filter canisters, worn as a hood or integrated into a helmet; grants full protection from inhaled toxins and gas (not just a resistance bonus) while canisters last, and is the standard companion piece to the Death Korps Flak Coat.\n\nLore: Full-face gas-sealed respirators, most iconically the Death Korps of Krieg's ubiquitous gas-hoods, are depicted in Forge World's Imperial Armour background and Krieg-focused Black Library fiction as standard kit for regiments that expect chemical, biological or simply toxic battlefield conditions as a matter of course.\n\nDesign: Sits a clear step above the Rebreather: full immunity rather than a resistance bonus, priced and weighted higher to reflect the canister filtration system, at the same \"standard Imperial manufacture\" TL as flak and carapace per framework.md's TL table.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "220",
			"base_weight": "3 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eh5J-v3xoW0lDg7Jp",
			"description": "Photo-Visor / Photo-Contacts",
			"local_notes": "Low-light image intensification, worn as a visor/goggle unit or, in the surgically-fitted \"photo-contacts\" variant, as lenses with no external bulk (same effect, -1 Holdout only for the visor version, none for contacts). Treat as granting Night Vision for game purposes while worn/active; does not help in absolute darkness.\n\nLore: Photo-visors (and their subtler photo-contact lens equivalent) are common-issue night-fighting gear across Astra Militarum and Inquisitorial-agent background, letting the wearer fight effectively in low light without a powered searchlight giving away their position.\n\nDesign: No anchor; modelled narratively as the GURPS Night Vision effect (Basic Set) for the GM to apply, since equipment-level features in this schema don't have a direct \"grant an advantage\" hook. Priced as inexpensive, widely issued optics rather than a rare relic.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "150",
			"base_weight": "1 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e_DAHhjQFqvQqe4gS",
			"description": "Magnoculars",
			"local_notes": "Long-range magnifying optics with image stabilisation and, on most patterns, a basic infrared overlay for spotting heat sources at range.\n\nLore: Standard-issue field optics for officers, snipers and scouts across Astra Militarum background, functionally binoculars with a targeting and threat-marking overlay fed to a user's other gear (vox, auspex) where available.\n\nDesign: No anchor; treated as GURPS binoculars (Basic Set) with an infrared spotting note added for setting flavour, at Imperial-standard TL10 manufacture.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "300",
			"base_weight": "2 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eIsYSeZNLhRnnL9wV",
			"description": "Auspex",
			"local_notes": "Handheld multi-sensor unit reading motion, heat, atmosphere and (on some patterns) psychic disturbance; the bonus above applies specifically to actively scanning an area, not passive Perception.\n\nLore: The auspex is the Imperium's ubiquitous field scanner, seen in nearly every piece of Inquisitorial, Astra Militarum and Adeptus Mechanicus fiction and background as the standard tool for detecting hidden enemies, structural hazards, or (in Inquisitorial hands) warp taint.\n\nDesign: No damage/armour anchor applies; modelled as a flat Search skill bonus (Basic Set's \"instruments help\" convention) rather than lifted from any Ultra-Tech scanner entry, priced as valuable, restricted military-grade kit (LC2) consistent with its usefulness and relative scarcity outside the Guard and Inquisition.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "2",
			"base_value": "900",
			"base_weight": "4 lb",
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "skill_bonus",
					"selection_type": "skills_with_name",
					"name": {
						"compare": "is",
						"qualifier": "Search"
					},
					"amount": 3
				}
			],
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eZoCqt4RE7UW5ir3C",
			"description": "Vox-Caster",
			"local_notes": "Backpack-mounted long-range radio, typically crewed by a dedicated vox-operator; range measured in miles rather than the short range of a micro-bead.\n\nLore: The vox-caster is the standard heavy communications set of Astra Militarum platoons and companies, a recurring detail in Guard-focused Black Library fiction (a vox-operator is a near-universal squad role) and Codex Astra Militarum background.\n\nDesign: No anchor; built as a straightforward GURPS long-range radio (Basic Set), weighted and priced to reflect a dedicated backpack set rather than a personal comm-bead.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "400",
			"base_weight": "12 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eYNILM6pHAA4qz7lw",
			"description": "Micro-bead",
			"local_notes": "Short-range personal earpiece/throat-mic comm unit for squad-level chatter; does not reach vox-caster range.\n\nLore: The micro-bead is standard personal-issue comms for individual Guardsmen and many other Imperial servants, referenced constantly in Black Library fiction as the earpiece through which squad orders and chatter travel.\n\nDesign: Short-range GURPS radio (Basic Set) at minimal weight and cost, deliberately much cheaper and shorter-ranged than the Vox-Caster to reflect the personal-vs-squad-support distinction the lore consistently draws between the two.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "80",
			"base_weight": "0.25 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "el2izu1rAyKYH-LQS",
			"description": "Preysense Goggles",
			"local_notes": "Combined thermal/motion overlay goggles, worn under a gas-hood visor; grants the same narrative Night Vision benefit as a photo-visor, plus motion-detection against concealed or camouflaged targets at the GM's discretion.\n\nLore: Preysense goggles are a signature piece of Death Korps of Krieg kit in Forge World's Imperial Armour background, combining thermal imaging with motion sensing to let Krieg troopers fight effectively through the toxic smoke and fog typical of their siege warfare.\n\nDesign: Built as an upgraded Photo-Visor (adds motion detection on top of low-light vision) rather than copied from any catalogue scanner; priced and weighted similarly to the Photo-Visor with a modest premium for the added sensor mode.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "350",
			"base_weight": "2 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eg_i-bGcc0igYOJ3Y",
			"description": "Grapnel Launcher",
			"local_notes": "Compressed-gas or micro-charge grapnel and line, used for scaling walls, boarding actions and rapid descent; treat as a GURPS grapnel-gun/pneumatic harpoon under Climbing rules.\n\nLore: Grapnel lines and launchers appear throughout Imperial special-operations depictions — Scions and other elite Guard, Sisters of Battle strike teams and Inquisitorial agents alike are shown using them for boarding actions and rapid vertical assault.\n\nDesign: No anchor; modelled as a standard GURPS grapnel tool (Basic Set climbing gear) rather than any catalogue \"descent device,\" priced and weighted for a compact personal-issue unit.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "3",
			"base_value": "250",
			"base_weight": "5 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "efG4QpvTdmi63DYl9",
			"description": "Jump Pack (Astartes)",
			"local_notes": "Rocket-propelled flight/leap pack used for short powered jumps and controlled descents in combat (the tabletop \"Assault\"/deep-strike leap); treat mechanically as a short-burst Flight capability the GM adjudicates per scene rather than sustained flight, with a real risk of burn damage to anyone caught in the exhaust wash on landing. Sized and rigged for the Astartes frame and its power-armour interface; an unaugmented human cannot use one safely.\n\nLore: Jump packs equip Assault Marines and Vanguard veterans across Codex Space Marines background and Black Library fiction, covering the ground between a standard Astartes's leap and true flight in short, thunderous bursts, at the cost of a very conspicuous, fuel-limited profile.\n\nDesign: No damage/armour anchor applies; the jump pack is deliberately left as a narrative/maneuver capability (short powered leaps, GM-adjudicated) rather than an automated GCS Flight bonus, since GURPS models tactical jump-pack flight as a maneuver, not a passive trait, and this schema has no clean equipment-level hook for that. Weight and cost scaled to sit well above mundane Guard equipment, in line with other Astartes-exclusive wargear in this file.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes"
			],
			"tech_level": "11",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "45000",
			"base_weight": "60 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e9gR3Jq0Hb_MMDtv7",
			"description": "Grav-chute",
			"local_notes": "Anti-grav-assisted parachute harness used for high-altitude insertion; converts what would otherwise be a lethal fall into a controlled descent.\n\nLore: Grav-chutes equip Guard airborne/drop formations (Elysian and similar regiments in Codex and Black Library background) for high-altitude, low-opening insertions from Valkyrie and Aquila lander drops.\n\nDesign: No anchor; modelled as a GURPS parachute/descent device (Basic Set) with a \"grav-assisted\" label for setting flavour, priced and weighted as specialist rather than universal Guard kit, hence the more restrictive LC2.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
			"tech_level": "10",
			"legality_class": "2",
			"base_value": "500",
			"base_weight": "8 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eQTDWnryCQYPawV7a",
			"description": "Medi-kit",
			"local_notes": "Standard combat first-aid kit; the bonus above applies to First Aid rolls made using it, per its contents (counter-septic, sealant foam, stimm shots, basic surgical tools).\n\nLore: Combat medi-kits are standard issue to Guard medicae and squad \"doc\" characters throughout Astra Militarum background and fiction, from field dressings to combat stimms.\n\nDesign: Direct translation of GURPS's standard first-aid kit bonus (Basic Set) rather than a Chirurgeon's full surgery kit, since this is field-carried personal gear rather than a hospital set.",
			"tags": [
				"Gear",
				"Imperium",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
